  84→    \"productName\": \"Cursor\",\n    85→    \"title\": \"Enterprise Model Controls and Usage Analytics\",\n    86→    \"description\": \"Enterprise admins can now set granular model access controls at provider and model level, with option to block new providers/versions by default. Soft spend limits and intelligent alerts prevent blocking users while maintaining visibility. Updated usage analytics allows filtering by users and product surfaces.\",\n    87→    \"updateType\": \"feature\",\n    88→    \"url\": \"https://cursor.com/changelog\",\n    89→    \"date\": \"2026-04-10\",\n    90→    \"community_feedback\": []\n    91→  },\n    92→  {\n    93→    \"productName\": \"Claude Code\",\n    94→    \"title\": \"Market Dominance and $30B ARR Achievement\",\n    95→    \"description\": \"As of May 2026, Claude Code has become the most-used AI coding tool, overtaking GitHub Copilot and Cursor in just eight months since its May 2025 release. Anthropic disclosed $30B ARR with 80x year-over-year growth. Captures 30% of CLI-based coding market and Google engineers have adopted it for daily workflows.\",\n    96→    \"updateType\": \"announcement\",\n    97→    \"url\": \"https://dev.to/alexmercedcoder/ai-weekly-claude-code-dominates-mcp-goes-mainstream-week-of-march-5-2026-15af\",\n    98→    \"date\": \"2026-05-08\",\n    99→    \"community_feedback\": [\n   100→      {\n   101→        \"quote\": \"Claude Code, released in May 2025, has become the most-used AI coding tool — overtaking both GitHub Copilot and Cursor in just eight months\",\n   102→        \"source\": \"Dev.to\",\n   103→        \"sentiment\": \"positive\"\n   104→      },\n   105→      {\n   106→        \"quote\": \"Anthropic disclosed $30B ARR (Annual Recurring Revenue) with 80x year-over-year growth\",\n   107→        \"source\": \"AI Dev News\",\n   108→        \"sentiment\": \"positive\"\n   109→      }\n   110→    ]\n   111→  }\n   112→]\n   113→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
